        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.591,
            0.89,
            0.728
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Holding the ends of the rubber band at A and B, place the stack of coins near the middle of A and B. Now, using a finger of your other hand, push back the stack of coins till the rubber band is pulled back to the mark C (Fig. 6.12c). Then, release the stack of coins and "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "observe",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " its motion. Do you find that after losing contact with the rubber band, the velocity of the stack of coins decreases gradually and it comes to rest after travelling some distance? Measure the distance travelled from C and record it. Repeat this step twice."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.73,
            0.89,
            0.815
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Repeat steps 3 and 4 for laminated table top while ensuring that the points A, B and C are marked at the same distances as earlier. Does the stack of coins travel a larger distance than it did on the wooden table top before coming to rest? Does its velocity decrease more slowly now?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.818,
            0.89,
            0.886
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Next, repeat step 5 on a horizontal polished marble or tile floor. Does the stack of coins travel an even larger distance and its velocity decrease even more slowly? What conclusion do you draw from your observations?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.889,
            0.89,
            0.925
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Before you release the stack of coins, it is stationary. It means that the forces acting upon it are balanced. Upon release, the force applied by you"
            }
